  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how time-resolved liquid-phase electron microscopy can be used to follow a reaction and infer its mechanism. A filmstrip-style sequence across the top shows clusters of mostly pink spheres changing over reaction progress as blue spheres appear and become incorporated into the particle groups. Early frames contain only pink clusters, while later frames show mixed pink–blue assemblies and a growing fraction of blue-containing products, indicating a gradual compositional transformation during the reaction.\n\nBelow the sequence, pie charts summarize this shift quantitatively: the red portion increases from left to right, suggesting that the amount of transformed or exchanged material rises as the reaction proceeds. An arrow labeled reaction progress ties the microscopy snapshots and summary charts into a time-evolution picture.\n\nOn the right, the figure connects these observations to a molecular-scale mechanism. A benzene-like ring with a blue substituent is shown converting, via an arrow, to a similar ring with a pink substituent, alongside a cluster and a dark dot that appears to represent the reactive particle or catalytic site. Overall, the image conveys a workflow from direct nanoscale monitoring of changing particle composition to a proposed atomistic or catalytic exchange mechanism.",
